WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.890
Film Courage: Your new book just came out
Now That’s Funny. I understand it’s 24

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:12.420
writers and you give them a premise, a situation
about a mother and daughter, instead of asking

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:17.869
them about their process because that’s
the fastest way to get fiction from an artist

00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:21.320
is to ask them what their creative process
is.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.880
Peter Desberg, Author: You know when you interview
somebody and you ask the about their process

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:31.640
you have no idea what you’re getting. And
a lot of people sort of romanticize the way

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:39.220
they wish their process was. Some will tell
you what they think they do. But the easiest

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.230
way to get at it (the most direct way ) is
to do it and we’ll watch. And these people

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:52.740
were so nice about letting us do that. And
one of the things that we thought was interesting

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:58.310
was the people we had had major credits. I
mean these were people who show runners, a

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:03.580
lot of them were show creators. All of them
at one point or another said “We’re kind

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:10.390
of nervous doing this. Because we have one
shot at this and then we are going to be compared

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:14.220
to really, really famous peers who will do
a better job.”

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.090
Film Courage: How much time did you give them
to work out this premise?

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:25.990
Peter Desberg: The average interview was anywhere
to an hour to two hours or so. And that was

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:26.990
it. They had their shot.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:37.500
Jeffrey Davis, Author: That you’re reading
in the book is liberally&nbsp;edited&nbsp;so the 

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.780
most important thing to us was the premise
making because you get 24 different stories

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:49.220
from one premise. But great questions come
out of it to but the most important thing

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:59.560
that we were sure not to cut their best work
and I think it comes across. And then the

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.130
little stories that we tell, the sidebars.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:13.590
Peter: I mean we tried to put some of our
own insights into it and every once and a

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:20.110
while we’d see something really unusual,
we’d see trends like a surprisedly number

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:25.420
of the writers had graduated degrees in math
and science. And it’s like “Huh? That’s

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.720
weird for comedy writers?” And one of the
things that we found interesting is particularly

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:37.420
for TV comedy writers (people in the room),
they tend to be more story guys or joke guys

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:42.180
and the science math guys are almost exclusively
story guys (they were well organized) and

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:47.270
they were really good at plotting. A lot of
the people we interviewed also had stand-up

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:51.580
backgrounds and they tended to be surprisingly
the joke guys.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:58.800
In fact there was one funny story that Lou
Schneider that wrote for Everybody Loves Raymond

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.280
told us a story that they were sitting around
the table and one of the guys grabs him and

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:09.250
pulls him under the table and he says “Pitch
this joke for me.” And he says “Pitch

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.650
it yourself.” And he says “No, you’ve
done stand-up. I want to make sure this gets

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.790
in. You’ll tell it better than me.”

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.930
Film Courage: So people knowing your skill
set.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:17.989
Peter: Yes.

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:22.430
Film Courage: What if we were to give you
a premise, our own premise? I’m curious

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.120
how the two of you would work it out?

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.340
Peter: It would be brilliant, I’m pretty
certain.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:37.319
Jeffrey: It would take a couple hours. Peter…feel
free to correct me…but my observation is

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:45.530
(I’ve talked about this a lot with my students)
is a good way to go out of a University (especially

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:51.360
as an undergrad) is with a partner. They are
much more likely to hire you with a partner

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:58.440
when you’re young and help you develop your
craft as a solo writer. I’m talking about

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.099
comedy. I don’t know anything about how
drama works. But I’m pretty sure that there

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:09.600
is so much cross-pollination now that it’s
the same. But the way Peter is…I got way

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:18.640
away from the story…I don’t know…what
am I on today, I have no idea?

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:25.770
I believe that it is really a bad thing for
people to have the same skills as their partners.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.639
This goes toward answering the premise question
you asked. If both people in the partnership

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:37.009
have the same skills, I don’t see a reason
for the partnership. Complementary skills…Peter

00:04:37.009 --> 00:04:43.720
has two things (one drives me crazy), he’s
able to put anything down in a draft, he has

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:51.820
no blocks. How he can even call himself a
writer without blocks is shameful. But he’ll

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:58.050
just put it down knowing it’s awful (it’s
not ever as awful as he thinks it is) and

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:05.099
then he’ll say to me “Can you work on
this and develop it?” Whereas I would rather

00:05:05.099 --> 00:05:11.740
have dental surgery than do a first draft.
It’s painful for me. But I love rewriting

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:21.520
but Peter is capable of seeing structure in
his head (I think that it comes from his science

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:28.050
background), I’ll start anywhere in the
story and just let it write itself. And both

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:33.379
of those ways of working on developing a premise
or anything else are valid.

00:05:33.379 --> 00:05:37.919
You know, one thing we’ve learned from this
book is there isn’t any one way to do it.

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:43.439
I don’t know if I answered your question,
but I like my answer better than your question.

00:05:43.439 --> 00:05:50.370
I mean I have a little blabbermouth going
on. Did that sort of answer the question?

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:57.289
Peter: We haven’t gotten to it yet. But
I will throw in one sort of interesting thing

00:05:57.289 --> 00:05:59.810
that we’ll get to. It will be foreplay for
the question.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:00.810
Film Courage: Oh good.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:04.389
Jeffrey: See he is trying to sell that book
[Peter’s other book].

00:06:04.389 --> 00:06:11.800
Peter: There was a really huge study done
at Berkeley years ago. And it was just creative

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:20.380
artists it was architects, doctors, all cross
every field. And the two major findings that

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:26.710
they came out with were creative people have
a better ability to tolerate ambiguity and

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:31.599
really importantly they have the ability to
suspend judgment. And one of the things you

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:36.069
find particularly with young writers and they’ll
write something and they’ll immediately

00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:43.689
get depressed saying it’s not good enough.
And one of the things that we’ve learned

00:06:43.689 --> 00:06:50.539
and that for sure helps me is that I want
to get something down. We again like the old

00:06:50.539 --> 00:06:52.909
Hemingway quote “Write drunk,&nbsp;edit&nbsp;sober.”

00:06:52.909 --> 00:06:56.759
So once you get something down then you have
something down then you have something to

00:06:56.759 --> 00:07:02.389
work on. And I’ve never had anything that
looks like at the end like it started out

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:08.729
at the beginning. And so I don’t feel constrained
writing anything because I know it’s going

00:07:08.729 --> 00:07:09.729
to get reshaped, reworked.

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:11.059
Jeffrey: I don’t have that.

